.claude/decisions.md:1102:What happened: after my B2 verification passed I applied my own mutant to `src/vtt/snippet-registry-runtime.ts` (uncommitted, +55/−11 from the B2 lane) and restored it with `git checkout -- <file>`. That restores the COMMITTED version (ed75b664), not the pre-mutant candidate; the lane's 55/11 change to that file was wiped from the worktree (status dropped from 8 files to 7; my own hash check printed "restored: NO", which is how I noticed). The standing memory rule "commit lane before any checkout; restore mutants by reversing the exact edit with a hash check, never by checkout" existed precisely for this and I broke it under time pressure. The mutant run itself produced no usable output (the grep pattern matched nothing), so nothing was learned from it either. Recovery: the lane log prints `git diff` blocks; the last `diff --git a/src/vtt/snippet-registry-runtime.ts` block was extracted and applied with `git apply` onto the committed version; success criterion = numstat 55/11 AND sha256 equal to the hash codex recorded for the file after its own mutant restorations (`d0b4d4ee…`). Result recorded in D789. Rule restated for myself: every supervisor mutant is applied and reversed by exact string substitution (python) with sha256 before/after, on a file whose pre-mutant hash is written down first; `git checkout`/`git stash` never touch a worktree with uncommitted lane work; when in doubt, commit the candidate FIRST (a commit can be amended or followed), then mutate.
