.claude/decisions.md:1425:## D852 — OWNER (2026-09-21 16:33): DATA-01 Opus judge hard cap = $400 total
.claude/decisions.md:1429:Consequences the DATA-01 fix r2 brief must carry: (1) at the recorded estimate (≈$0.10 per judge call at opus 4.8 list price, ≈10k in / ≈2k out) $400 buys ≈3,700–4,000 single-call states after an 8 % failure reserve, i.e. FEWER states than the $500 Luna cap can label (≈7,150); the plan must therefore either score all of a state's D846 tuning actions in ONE judge call (supervisor's recommendation) and size the judged corpus to the cap, or state which states go unjudged and how the training unit treats rows without an Opus column; (2) if the pilot's real receipts show a per-state cost that would leave part of the Luna-labelled corpus unjudged, the plan reports the shortfall to the owner rather than trimming judge coverage silently (D845: judges cover every labelled state); (3) judge prompt versioned, blinded, engine ranking withheld (D845 §2) — unchanged.
.claude/decisions.md:1431:Supervisor correction recorded at full length: in the 14:4x report the supervisor described the DATA-01 judge panel as "fable, astra, sol" (that is the plan-review panel of D587.1, not the D844 dataset judges = opus high + sol high) and claimed $750 gave "about 40 % headroom" over the full state set; at the D845 estimate $750 vs ≈$715 is ≈5 %. Both were supervisor errors, corrected before the owner ruled.
.claude/decisions.md:1433:Next owner question (one at a time): DATA-01 plan Q3 — collect primary/fallback labels only, or also the adjustment (retain/replace/replacement) labels? Next free id: D853.
.claude/decisions.md:1439:## D853 — OWNER (2026-09-21 17:14): DATA-01 collects adjustment decisions in v1 — retain/replace AND the reason
.claude/decisions.md:1441:Verbatim: "Adjustments are important. We need to know if the plan needed to be altered and why". Ruling as read (overrides plan §10 Q3's recommendation of primary/fallback first): (a) `adjustment_disposition` (retain|replace) and `adjustment_replacement` are PAID, collected labels in the first dataset, not a stub gated for later; (b) each adjustment label must also capture WHY — the teacher's stated reason for altering (or keeping) the plan, recorded as a typed field alongside the disposition, and the typed materiality inputs the engine already computes (what changed between commit and execution) are stored as features so "needed to be altered" is answerable from the row, not only from the teacher's prose; (c) adjustment states are captured wherever the engine reaches an adjustment boundary (archive, capture-only, teacher-continuation, branch), not synthesized; (d) corrections (malformed-submission repair) stay out of v1 (plan §2, unchanged); (e) cost: adjustment calls draw on the same $500 Luna cap (D841) and $400 Opus starting cap (D852/D852.1); the fix r2 brief must give the projected split between primary/fallback and adjustment calls and the resulting state count, and report to the owner if full adjustment coverage would push either cap. Supervisor note for fix r2: the "reason" field needs its own blinded judge criterion (is the stated reason consistent with the materiality inputs?) so the judges (D844/D845) score it too. Next owner question: the state-count vs tuning-count trade-off (D846). Next free id: D854.
.claude/decisions.md:1443:## D854 — OWNER (2026-09-21 17:44): DATA-01 repetition is set by statistical significance, blinded — states follow from the cap after that
.claude/decisions.md:1445:Verbatim: "Make sure it is enough blind repetition that it is statistically significant" (answer to the state-count vs tuning-count trade-off). Rulings as read: (a) the number of Luna samples per state per tuning is NOT a fixed k chosen by fiat (the plan's k=5); it is set by a preregistered significance criterion the plan must state — e.g. the chosen top option must be separable from the runner-up at a stated confidence before a hard label is issued, and a state whose samples do not reach it stays none-dominant or receives more samples up to a stated ceiling (sequential sampling with a stop rule is acceptable if the rule is fixed before collection); (b) the same applies to tuning comparisons: a claim that tuning X beats tuning Y on a state must rest on enough engine rollout seeds that the difference in simulated win probability is significant at the stated level, so the 32-seed rollout count is to be justified or replaced by a seed count derived from the criterion; (c) every repetition is BLIND: option order shuffled per sample, actor/option identities hidden, no sample sees another sample's answer, the engine's recommendation withheld (D845); (d) the trade-off is resolved in this order: fix repetition by the significance criterion first, then the number of tunings (D846's small fixed set), then the state count is whatever the $500 Luna cap (D841) and $400 Opus starting cap (D852.1) buy — fewer, well-replicated states over more, under-replicated ones; (e) the pilot (200 states) reports the achieved separation and the samples it took, so the criterion's cost is known from receipts before scale; if the projected state count falls below what the training unit needs, report to the owner (the caps are revisable, D852.1) rather than loosening the criterion. Fix r2 brief must carry (a)–(e) with the exact test, confidence level and stop rule named by the plan and attacked by astra. Next owner question: the brainstorm's three housekeeping questions (gate-config umbrella under D842; generalizing the D583 digest exception; baseline-of-record). Next free id: D855.
