     1	# Supervision standing rules
     2	
     3	These are the rules the autonomous build loop runs under. They used to live only
     4	in the `/loop` tick brief, which meant they were re-sent — and re-read, and
     5	re-billed — on every single tick, and evaporated between sessions. They are
     6	durable now. **The tick brief must point here, never copy from here.**
     7	
     8	Pointers, not copies, for things that already have a home:
     9	
    10	| What | Where it actually lives |
    11	|---|---|
    12	| Binding decisions and findings; **wins over every other file** | `.claude/decisions.md` |
    13	| How to report (terse for reports, rich for questions) | `CLAUDE.md` § How to report |
    14	| Never state a D&D rule from memory; the two-column SRD trap | `AGENTS.md` |
    15	| Open questions for the owner | `.claude/pending-questions/` |
    16	
    17	## Model policy
    18	
    19	Delegate to codex `-p sol` (preferred), `-p sol-medium`, `-p terra` (fallback
    20	only). Prompts **always** via stdin files, never inline. Opus is scarce.
    21	
    22	**Codex out of credits → STOP LOUDLY.** Never substitute a Claude agent for the
    23	codex role.
    24	
    25	Sonnet is authorized for **KB lookups only**, as the second reader in
    26	`kb_ask.py`. The reason is accuracy, not cost. Hard boundary: sonnet reads the
    27	KB, sonnet decides nothing.
    28	
    29	## Effort
    30	
    31	**Default is `high`, globally, and that is deliberate.** Fable in particular
    32	stays at `high`. Effort is decided per dispatch, not lowered as a blanket
    33	policy.
    34	
    35	`effortLevel` in `settings.json` is a **single global value** — there is no
    36	per-model map (no `effortByModel` anywhere in the CLI). "Fable high, Opus
    37	medium" cannot be expressed as a setting. The levers that do exist:
    38	
    39	| Lever | Scope |
    40	|---|---|
    41	| `/effort <low\|medium\|high\|xhigh\|max\|auto>` | current session, interactive |
    42	| `claude --effort <level>` | one launched process |
    43	| `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL=<level>` | session-only, overrides settings, saves nothing |
    44	| `Workflow` → `agent({effort})` | one subagent call |
    45	| `Agent` tool | **no effort parameter** — inherits the session |
    46	
    47	**Opus runs at `medium`; Fable runs at `high`.** Opus 5 at medium has a
    48	measured median turn time of **2.3s against 4.4s at high** (n=200 / n=16,578
    49	across every local transcript) — roughly 1.9x faster, with lower reasoning
    50	cost as the mechanism.
    51	
    52	I previously claimed medium and high were "the same configuration" on Opus 5.
    53	That was wrong and the correction matters more than the claim did:
    54	
    55	- The shared field is `cell:"o5-bmin"`, which is the **harness-side budget
    56	  hint**, not the effort sent to the model. The dispatcher is
    57	  `ZZl(e,t){let r=oQr[e][t]; return r.modelEffort==="typed" ? t : r.modelEffort}`
    58	  — and on Opus 5 both levels carry `modelEffort:"typed"`, so the function
    59	  returns the level string itself. `medium` sends *medium*; `high` sends
    60	  *high*. They differ in precisely the field that controls model reasoning.
    61	- The evidence I used could not have detected the difference. I compared
    62	  `output_tokens`, but **0% of these records carry a thinking block** —
    63	  reasoning content is not in the transcripts at all. Effort scales reasoning
    64	  tokens; that measurement was blind to the quantity in dispute.
    65	- Latency was in the same records the whole time and points the other way.
    66	
    67	Reasoning-token cost cannot be measured from local transcripts. Absence of a
    68	measurement is not evidence of absence of an effect — the 2.3s/4.4s split is
    69	the observable that survives.
    70	
    71	Since `effortLevel` is global per session, the policy is a switch, not a map:
    72	`/effort medium` for Opus work, `/effort high` when driving Fable.
    73	
    74	## Cadence
    75	
    76	The prompt cache TTL is **one hour**, measured from the last request. Measured
    77	across 48,643 real inter-request gaps in this machine's transcripts: below
    78	3600s the cache-miss rate is 0.7–4.6%; at 3600s and above it is 78% and median
    79	`cache_read` drops to literally zero while `cache_write` jumps to ~331k.
    80	
    81	`ScheduleWakeup` clamps `delaySeconds` to [60, 3600], so **every allowed delay
    82	is inside the window** — but 3600 sits exactly on the cliff. Use **3300s** for
    83	an idle heartbeat: maximum quiet, with headroom for jitter and for the tick's
    84	own runtime.
    85	
    86	Do not schedule short wakeups to poll harness-tracked work. Completion
    87	re-invokes the loop automatically; a short poll is pure waste. Short delays are
    88	only for external state the harness cannot see (CI, a deploy, a remote queue).
    89	
    90	Caveat: in usage overage the TTL drops to five minutes. Re-measure before
    91	trusting the number above if the account has gone into overage.
    92	
    93	## Verification, non-delegable
    94	
    95	Run the gates yourself and paste real numbers. Every load-bearing new assertion
    96	gets a negative control: apply, **prove applied**, run, revert, **prove
    97	restoration** by re-running.
    98	
    99	- Compile gate is `npx tsc -b --force`. Root `tsconfig.json` is a solution with
   100	  references to `tsconfig.app.json` and `tsconfig.node.json`; build mode follows
   101	  both, so application code plus tests and tooling are checked.
   102	  `npx tsc -p tsconfig.app.json --noEmit` is app-only and is not the gate.
   103	  Ordinary project mode (`npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit`) checks no source
   104	  files with this root `files: []` solution configuration; use build mode.
   105	- Never read an exit code through a pipe.
   106	- Run gates under load and let lanes keep flowing. A timeout red gets one serial
   107	  rerun under the gate lock before it counts; never re-pin a budget from load.
   108	  Timeout increases remain limited to tests explicitly named by D544/D606/D613.
   109	- Full Vitest, Playwright, and production builds remain serialized through
   110	  `/tmp/dnd-gate.lock`; run no Vitest while Playwright owns that lock. See
   111	  `.claude/decisions.md` Part A (Verification, gates and evidence) for the surviving lock boundary.
   112	- Browser work still uses a unique port; never use reserved port 4173.
   113	- Fresh worktrees need `npm ci`.
   114	- `git commit` always `-F msgfile`. Commit by **explicit path**.
   115	- Python, not shell, for text checks — the shell mangles `**`, backticks and `$`.
   116	
   117	### Controlling decisions and executable sources
   118	
   119	- Compile scope and command: D263 requires build mode; the 2026-09-03 14:30
   120	  supervisor finding requires `--force`. Project references are executable in
   121	  [`../tsconfig.json`](../tsconfig.json); `.claude/decisions.md` Part A records the
   122	  forced compile command and supervisor finding.
   123	- Gate load, retry, and timeout policy: D587.3, with named exceptions in D544,
   124	  D606, and D613; serialization and the locked retry remain in
   125	  `.claude/decisions.md` Part A. The newest entry in
   126	  [`decisions.md`](decisions.md) wins on conflict.
   127	- Required handoff gate inventory (M-3 owner):
   128	  [`REQUIRED_HANDOFF_GATES`](../tools/vtt-handoff/report.ts). Do not duplicate
   129	  that generated command list here.
   130	
   131	## Forbidden paths to green
   132	
   133	Never tune to pass. Budgets are law. No weakened assertions. No `.skip`,
   134	`.todo`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, or `any`. No config edits to reach
   135	green. Never delete a test to pass. **Never regenerate an expectation from our
   136	own output.**
   137	
   138	A validator that refuses everything is not a fix. A guard is proven by
   139	executing it, never by a pasted claim.
   140	
   141	## Hard stops
   142	
   143	1. **Licensing (D59).** Copyrighted or derived content lives in the private repo
   144	   only — never an artifact, never public. SRD 5.2.1 is CC-BY and is the
   145	   exception. A public doc may state a licensing gate *outcome* but never the
   146	   audit evidence.
   147	2. **Destroying work.** History rewrite, force-push, bulk deletion.
   148	   `.claude/decisions.md` is supervisor-only; new decisions append in its Part D (compacted 2026-09-18, D682; full chronology in `.claude/decisions-archive/`).
   149	3. **Outward-facing actions.** Push, publish, send. Local commits are not
   150	   outward-facing.
   151	
   152	Data loss is **not** a stop condition (D60) — v1 has zero users. That does not
   153	license shipping an export its own importer refuses.
   154	
   155	Never commit into the owner's work repo while their index is loaded.
   156	
   157	`KILL BY PID, NEVER BY PATTERN.` Owner codex PIDs 2764 and 2771 are never
   158	touched. Never use `pgrep`.
   159	
   160	## Failure lessons — all of them mine
   161	
   162	1. **Pre-merge:** `git status --porcelain` zero, and the tip must contain a
   163	   signature symbol from the fix.
   164	2. Never read an exit code through a pipe.
   165	3. Never state a D&D rule from recall. The two-column SRD defeats plain grep
   166	   *silently* — and it defeated our own extractor the same way.
   167	4. Sweep **all** files after correcting a claim; correct the governing copy first.
   168	5. Never point a generator at a directory a lane owns.
   169	6. **A mid-flight state is not a verdict. A completion notification is not a
   170	   verdict either.**
   171	7. Deletion proves absence; the failure that ships is a **well-formed wrong
   172	   value**. A passing mutation can mean the test is vacuous.
   173	8. Truncated listings are not evidence of absence. Nor is a missing object key.
   174	9. **My validators over-refused five times**, every one caught only by a
   175	   valid-case control. Every matcher change needs a lawful-payload accept case.
   176	10. Kill by PID, never by pattern.
   177	11. Measure the baseline yourself before briefing it.
   178	12. Baseline a repo before dispatching into it.
   179	13. **Skipped is not passed, and exit 0 lies.**
   180	14. Never commit into the owner's work repo when their index is loaded.
   181	15. Do not inflate the mistake register either.
   182	16. Check whether the maintenance doc is teaching the defect.
   183	17. A sandbox can fake a failure.
   184	18. **A fix can introduce the inverse bug.** Round 7's alias fix began merging
   185	    distinct pools. After every "we now reject X", ask what lawful thing is now
   186	    wrongly rejected or merged.
   187	19. A brief that is re-sent every tick is re-billed every tick. This file exists
   188	    because of that.
   189	
   190	## Roles (D5/D6)
   191	
   192	Opus writes plans with the full assumption list, provable ones proven *before*
   193	the plan is final. Independent review, cap 3 rounds — except new subsystems,
   194	which are reviewed **until clean, no cap** (D247).
   195	
   196	Pin the contract in the seam file (`src/builder/contracts.ts`). Production and
   197	test agents work in parallel and neither owns the seam. Ratify the
   198	implementer's value *before* tests are written, never after.
   199	
   200	The supervisor gates, verifies and merges. That is never delegated.
   201	
   202	## Merge ritual
   203	
   204	worktree clean → full vitest (**read the verdict**) → `npm run build` →
   205	Playwright on a unique port, expect 170 → codex review → `merge-to-main.sh` →
   206	post-merge vitest → `git push mirror main` → prune.
   207	20. **Never mutate a worktree while its own suite is running.** A background
   208	    full-suite run collects files as they are on disk mid-run; a mutation
   209	    cycle raced against it produces failures that look like the fix's and are
   210	    yours. The one-suite rule applies to the supervisor's own runs too:
   211	    mutation cycles and full suites SERIALIZE in a worktree.

